        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_needle_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "Introduction and definition",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_needle_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A surgical needle is a sharp, specially designed instrument used to pass suture material through tissues during wound closure, ligation, repair and anastomosis."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is designed to penetrate tissue with minimal trauma while providing sufficient strength and control for accurate suturing."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Surgical needles may be supplied separately or, more commonly in modern practice, attached to a suture thread by a manufactured junction."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key OSCE distinction:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Needle: Penetrates tissue and guides the suture through it."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Suture: The thread used to approximate tissue or ligate structures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Needle holder: Holds and controls the needle during suturing."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Atraumatic needle: Usually has suture material attached to the needle by a swaged junction, minimising the tissue trauma caused by a separate eye."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_needle_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "Identification and general features",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_needle_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_needle_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "slideshare.net"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A typical curved surgical needle has the following components:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Part",
                    "Description and function"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Point",
                      "The leading end that penetrates tissue."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Body or shaft",
                      "The main section grasped by the needle holder."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Curvature",
                      "Determines the arc through which the needle passes."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Swaged end",
                      "Junction where the suture is attached in an atraumatic needle."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Eye, if present",
                      "Opening through which suture material is threaded in a conventional eyed needle."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Cross-sectional profile",
                      "Influences penetration, cutting behaviour and tissue trauma."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "In an OSCE, describe the needle's curvature, point type, body cross-section and whether it is eyed or swaged."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_needle_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "Classification by needle curvature",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_needle_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_needle_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "A. Straight needle"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Used where the needle can be manipulated directly by hand or where a straight approach is appropriate, such as selected superficial suturing procedures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_needle_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "B. One-quarter circle (¼ circle)"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Suitable for selected superficial or relatively accessible tissues, depending on the needle design."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_needle_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "C. Three-eighths circle (⅜ circle)"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Commonly used for skin, subcutaneous tissue and many general surgical suturing tasks."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_needle_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "D. Half circle (½ circle)"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Useful for suturing in deeper or more confined operative spaces, including selected gastrointestinal and genitourinary procedures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_needle_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "E. Five-eighths circle (⅝ circle)"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Used for selected deep or confined spaces where a greater arc is useful."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The curvature is selected according to tissue accessibility, the required needle trajectory and the surgeon's technique. These are general patterns rather than rigid rules."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_needle_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "Classification by needle point",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_needle_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This is one of the highest-yield surgical instrument OSCE topics. The point determines how the needle penetrates tissue and the type of tissue for which it is suitable."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_needle_10.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "A. Taper-point needle"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The point is rounded and gradually narrows to a sharp tip. It separates tissue fibres rather than cutting a slit."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses: Bowel, peritoneum, muscle, blood vessels and other relatively soft tissues, depending on the procedure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_needle_11.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "B. Conventional cutting needle"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Has a triangular cutting cross-section, with the cutting edge on the inner curvature of the needle."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses: Selected tough tissues, particularly skin."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_needle_12.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "C. Reverse-cutting needle"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Has its principal cutting edge on the outer curvature. This design helps resist tissue cut-through compared with a conventional cutting needle in appropriate applications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses: Skin, tough connective tissue and selected plastic surgical procedures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_needle_13.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "D. Tapercut needle"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Combines a cutting tip with a tapering body."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses: Selected dense or fibrous tissues where initial penetration is difficult but a less cutting body is desirable."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_needle_14.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "E. Blunt-point needle"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Has a rounded, non-cutting tip."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses: Selected friable tissues, such as the liver or kidney, where reducing accidental needle-stick injury and tissue penetration is desirable."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_needle_15.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "F. Spatula needle"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Has a flattened profile designed to pass through specific tissue planes."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Uses: Ophthalmic surgery, particularly selected corneal and scleral procedures."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_needle_group_6",
              "group_title": "High-yield comparison",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Needle type",
                    "Key distinguishing feature",
                    "Typical application"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Taper point",
                      "Rounded, gradually narrowing point",
                      "Soft tissues and hollow viscera"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Conventional cutting",
                      "Cutting edge on inner curvature",
                      "Selected tough tissues"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Reverse cutting",
                      "Cutting edge on outer curvature",
                      "Skin and dense connective tissue"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tapercut",
                      "Cutting tip with tapering body",
                      "Dense or fibrous tissue"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Blunt point",
                      "Rounded, blunt tip",
                      "Friable tissues"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Spatula",
                      "Flattened profile",
                      "Ophthalmic surgery"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Exam pearl: Do not confuse a cutting needle with a taper-point needle. Cutting needles slice through dense tissue, whereas taper-point needles primarily separate tissue fibres."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_needle_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "Classification by attachment and construction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_needle_group_7",
              "group_title": "A. Eyed needle",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_needle_16.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A traditional needle with an eye through which the surgeon threads the suture."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Requires manual threading."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The thread and needle pass together through tissue."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The doubled thread at the eye can produce a larger tissue tract."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Less common in many modern surgical settings than swaged needles."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_needle_group_8",
              "group_title": "B. Swaged needle",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_needle_17.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The suture is permanently attached to the needle at the proximal end by a manufactured junction."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Common in modern sterile suture packs."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Reduces tissue trauma associated with a separate needle eye."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Simplifies handling and preparation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Often supplied as a single-use sterile needle-suture unit."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_needle_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "Needle holder and correct handling",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_needle_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_needle_18.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_needle_19.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/surgical_needle_20.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A needle holder is a surgical instrument used to grasp, control and drive the needle through tissue."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Common types include:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Mayo-Hegar: General-purpose needle holder."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Crile-Wood: Fine needle holder for delicate suturing."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Castroviejo: Fine, often spring-handled needle holder used in microsurgery and ophthalmic procedures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Olsen-Hegar: Combines a needle holder with scissors for cutting suture material."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_needle_group_10",
              "group_title": "Correct needle-holder technique",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Select a needle holder appropriate for the needle size and procedure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Grasp the needle securely around the junction of its middle and posterior thirds, or at the manufacturer's recommended grasping point."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Avoid grasping the extreme tip or swaged junction."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Use the needle's curvature to drive it through tissue by rotating the wrist rather than pushing straight through with excessive force."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Avoid repeatedly clamping the same delicate area, which may deform or weaken the needle."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Inspect for bending, damage or loss of needle integrity."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The precise grasping point depends on the needle design and the manufacturer's guidance."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_needle_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "Properties of an ideal surgical needle",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_needle_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An ideal surgical needle should have:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Sharpness: Penetrates tissue with minimal force."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Adequate strength: Resists bending under normal use."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Ductility: Allows some deformation before breaking, depending on the material and design."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Corrosion resistance: Maintains integrity during manufacture, sterilisation and intended use."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Appropriate curvature: Permits controlled passage through the target tissue."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Secure suture attachment: Minimises separation between the needle and thread."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Minimal tissue trauma: Produces the smallest practical tissue tract for the intended suture."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Good handling characteristics: Can be controlled accurately with the appropriate needle holder."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Most modern surgical needles are made from high-strength surgical stainless steel or other suitable medical-grade alloys."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_needle_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "Selection of a needle for different tissues",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_needle_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Tissue or procedure",
                    "Commonly suitable needle",
                    "Reason"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Skin",
                      "Reverse-cutting or conventional cutting",
                      "Penetrates relatively tough tissue"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Bowel",
                      "Taper point",
                      "Separates soft tissue fibres"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Blood vessels",
                      "Fine taper-point needle",
                      "Helps minimise trauma to the vessel wall"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Liver or other friable tissue",
                      "Blunt-point needle in selected procedures",
                      "May reduce tissue tearing and accidental needle-stick risk"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tendon or dense fibrous tissue",
                      "Cutting or tapercut, depending on the procedure",
                      "Facilitates penetration of dense tissue"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Cornea or sclera",
                      "Fine spatula or specialised ophthalmic needle",
                      "Designed for delicate ocular tissue planes"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Subcutaneous tissue",
                      "Usually taper point or another suitable tissue-specific design",
                      "Allows passage through soft tissue"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Bone or heavily calcified tissue",
                      "Specialised instruments and techniques are required",
                      "Ordinary soft-tissue needles are not designed for routine bone penetration"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Needle choice must also take account of the suture material, needle diameter, tissue thickness and the procedure being performed."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_needle_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "Advantages and disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_needle_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Advantages",
                    "Disadvantages"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Allows precise passage of sutures",
                      "Needle-stick injuries can transmit blood-borne pathogens"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Different designs suit different tissues",
                      "Incorrect selection may cause tissue trauma"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Swaged needles minimise trauma associated with a separate eye",
                      "Needles can bend or break"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Curved needles allow access to confined spaces",
                      "Curved needles may be difficult to control in superficial spaces"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Modern needles are strong and corrosion-resistant",
                      "Cutting needles can cause tissue cut-through if used incorrectly"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Enables closure, ligation and reconstruction",
                      "Needles must be handled and discarded safely"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_needle_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "Complications and hazards",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_needle_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "A. Needle-stick injury"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Accidental penetration can expose healthcare workers to blood-borne pathogens. Immediate reporting and the relevant occupational exposure protocol are essential."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "B. Tissue trauma"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "An inappropriate needle point or excessive force can cause tearing, bleeding or damage to delicate structures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "C. Needle breakage"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Excessive force, repeated bending or inappropriate handling can cause the needle to break. A missing needle fragment requires immediate communication and appropriate localisation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "checklist",
                  "items": [
                    "D. Suture-needle separation"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Failure of the swaged junction or damage to the attachment may leave the needle detached from the suture, complicating the procedure."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_needle_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "Safety precautions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_needle_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Use a needle holder rather than fingers to manipulate curved needles whenever appropriate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Avoid passing sharp needles hand-to-hand when a neutral-zone technique can be used."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Count needles and other sharps according to the surgical counting protocol."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Do not attempt to straighten a bent needle for reuse."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Dispose of needles in an appropriate sharps container."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Do not recap, bend or deliberately break contaminated needles by hand."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "If a needle is missing or broken, notify the surgical team immediately and follow the retained-item protocol."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "surgical_needle_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "High-yield OSCE viva questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "surgical_needle_group_16",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What are the main parts of a surgical needle?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The point, body or shaft, curvature and attachment end. An eyed needle has an eye, while a swaged needle has a manufactured suture attachment."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is the difference between a taper-point and a reverse-cutting needle?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A taper-point needle separates tissue fibres using a gradually narrowing point. A reverse-cutting needle has a cutting edge on the outer curvature and is commonly used for skin and dense connective tissue."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What is a swaged needle?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A needle with suture material attached directly to its proximal end by a manufactured junction."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. Why is a reverse-cutting needle often used for skin?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Its cutting-edge configuration provides effective penetration of tough tissue and can reduce the tendency for the suture to cut through the wound edge compared with a conventional cutting needle in appropriate circumstances."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. Which needle holder is used for microsurgery?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A Castroviejo needle holder is commonly used in ophthalmic and other microsurgical procedures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. What should you do if a surgical needle breaks?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Inform the surgeon immediately, maintain control of the operative field, identify the missing fragment and follow the surgical retained-item protocol. Do not continue blindly if a fragment may remain in the patient."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Why should you avoid grasping the needle tip with a needle holder?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It can damage the point, reducing sharpness and increasing the force required for penetration. Grasping at an inappropriate location may also deform or weaken the needle."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
